group: C3v
formula: NH3
construction: a central atom and its neighbours
headline: 
source: MO levels: RHF, def2-SVP · 
note: Model: order and approximate spacing from extended Hückel (qualitative) · calculation in eV · electrons: N 5 (neutral: 3 unpaired e), drawn 5; H3 3 (neutral: 3 unpaired e), drawn 3; model and calculation: 8 drawn + 0 folded (0 levels) = 8 · labels in C<sub>3v</sub>\n1 high empty level drawn at the top (not to scale); 2 levels have an overlap below 0.80 with the matched calculated orbital
about: The extended Hückel diagram explains the calculation's orbitals: nothing more, nothing less.\n\n**The model.** Every column is extended Hückel (Alvarez parameters). The middle column is the molecule's own extended Hückel run, the side columns are the two fragments' (N and H3). The basis is the same in all three, so the lines are an exact projection of each model MO on the fragment orbitals: a line joins a model MO to a fragment level that makes up at least 10 % of it.\n\n**Bonding and antibonding** are read from the sign of each model MO's overlap population between the two fragments: above +0.02 bonding, below -0.02 antibonding (*), otherwise non-bonding. A bonding and an antibonding level of the same degeneracy that are the in-phase and out-of-phase combinations of the same fragment orbitals are joined as partners and share a colour; a level without such a partner keeps its own classification.\n\n**Scale.** The model columns show the order of the levels and their approximate spacing from extended Hückel, not a scale: close levels are drawn further apart so they can be read, and no model energy is printed (it is in the hover text). Extended Hückel antibonding energies are qualitative and typically far too high for short bonds to diffuse atoms such as B, Be and Al.\n\n**The calculation** is the column on the right, on its own energy axis in eV (not on the model's scale). For each model level it shows the calculated orbital (or set of near-degenerate orbitals) with the largest overlap with it, at its calculated energy, in the colour of the model level it matches. Beside each matched level, "overlap" is |<model MO|calculated MO>| (a number from 0 to 1); values below 0.80 are counted in the note under the diagram. Labels are those of the matched calculated orbital. Clicking a level shows that orbital in 3-D.\n\nThe model explains the calculated occupied orbitals in the right order. Virtual orbitals above the LUMO depend strongly on the basis set.\n\n**Total electrons: 8** (N 5, H3 3).\n\nEach model level carries a short grey tag naming the fragment orbitals it is mostly made of (shares of at least 20 %), such as Cr d or CO π*. Ligand-field view hides empty ligand-centred levels such as CO π*; transitions into them are charge transfer, not d–d.\n\n**Caveats.** 1 high empty level drawn at the top (not to scale); 2 levels have an overlap below 0.80 with the matched calculated orbital. Extended Hückel puts empty antibonding orbitals tens of eV up, so an empty level more than 30 eV above the HOMO is drawn at the top of the diagram, marked with an up arrow, or not at all when it sits almost entirely on one fragment. A loose match means no calculated orbital resembles the model level closely; read its calculated energy with care.\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in C3v (cross-checked against the code's own CS labels, which agreed on all 29 orbitals both sides could label, and every computed label subduces to match).
verdict: differs The model explains the calculated occupied orbitals in the right order. The empty levels differ: the calculated LUMO is 4a1, the model's is 3e*.
